  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 349 AM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nBroad high pressure continues to dominate the weather front across \nthe lower 48 leading to tranquil weather conditions.  Good radiative \nconditions has allows patchy fog to develop generally confined to \nlow lying areas. Because the fog is expected to remain patchy, have \nnot considered a dense fog advisory. Any fog is expected to burn off \nwithin a few hours of sunrise. A weak cold front associated with a \nlow pressure system over Ontario is expected to approach from the \nnorth today.  A few high clouds may filter into northern Missouri \nthis morning, but as they continue to push southeast in northwest \nflow they encounter more dry air limiting cloud cover today. As \nmixing increases today, westerly-northwesterly winds will mix-down \nfrom aloft effectively washing out the boundary.  With 925 hpa \ntemperatures nearly 10 degrees C warmer than yesterday and mostly \nclear skies, expect a larger diurnal curve today with high \ntemperatures in the 60s F. \n\nSurface ridge reorients itself over the Tennessee River Valley \ntonight into Monday leading to southwest flow across the region \nleading to a gradual warming trend Monday. Another weak cold front \napproaches from the north on Tuesday leading to potential slight \ncooling across northern Missouri.  \n\nA series of waves building southeast attempt to phase into a \ndeepening trough mid-week.  Depending on how quickly this occurs, \nportions of central Missouri may see the potential for rain showers \nalong a cold front building south.  Cooler conditions follow late \nweek with the potential for more frost/freeze conditions Friday and \nSaturday mornings.  Temperatures trend back above seasonal norms \nlate next weekend as trough along the east coast shifts east and \nupper level ridge builds into the central US.",
